alias Script = pkg::value::Script;
alias Bases = pkg::value::Bases;
alias Heap = pkg::heap::Heap;

public struct CompileError {
  public line: u64;
  public message: Box<Slots<u8>>;
}

public fn compile(heap: &Heap, chunk_name: &[u8], source: &[u8], bases: Bases) -> result_value: Result<Script, CompileError> reads(chunk_name), reads(source), writes(heap) doc "PUC Lua 5.1 one-pass parser and expression-descriptor code generator. Prototypes are numbered in lexical preorder, main first, and code and constants occupy contiguous ranges; code positions, constant bases, prototype numbers (Closure cells included) and debug-name offsets are absolute from the given bases, which are all zero for a script that runs alone and the sizes of the program for one appended to it. LoadKx indexes relative to the prototype kbase. Fused comparisons mean: if (cmp(b, c) == a) jump to target else fall through. TestJmp jumps when truth(R(a)) equals c; TestSetJmp assigns R(a) = R(b) and jumps when truth(R(b)) equals c. TForLoop jumps when the first generator result is nonnil. Closure is followed by one capture pseudo-cell per upvalue: Move(a: 0, b: local register), or GetUpval(a: 0, u: enclosing upvalue index), consumed by the VM rather than dispatched. lineinfo has one entry per emitted cell, retaining the first instruction's PUC line for fused pairs. Debug names are script-owned bytes outside the Lua heap; local lifetime boundaries use the same absolute cell map as jumps, and upvalue names retain capture order.";
